 ### [Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold)](13009-early-ray-marching-termination-threshold.md)
-Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold) is an optimization that halts the ray marching loop when the accumulated transmission falls below a user-defined small value, thereby skipping the computation of negligible volume contributions. It trades statistical accuracy for performance by discarding the "long tail" of the volume's contribution. Unlike standard constant step marching, which samples until exit or hit, this method stops early when further contributions are deemed insignificant. See [Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold)](13009-early-ray-marching-termination-threshold.md).
+Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold) is an optimization that halts the ray marching loop when the accumulated transmission falls below a user-defined small value, thereby skipping the computation of negligible volume contributions [G]. It trades statistical accuracy for performance by discarding the "long tail" of the volume's contribution [G]. Unlike standard constant step marching, which samples until exit or hit, this method stops early when further contributions are deemed insignificant [G]. Choose this method for volumetric rendering when performance is prioritized over strict physical accuracy, allowing early exit when transmission drops below a threshold. See [Early Ray Marching Termination (Threshold)](13009-early-ray-marching-termination-threshold.md).
 
